WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.617
- I've made billions
of dollars of failures,

00:00:03.617 --> 00:00:04.670
at Amazon.com.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.544
Literally billions of dollars of failures.

00:00:07.544 --> 00:00:09.752
It's easy to have ideas;

00:00:09.752 --> 00:00:13.572
it's very hard to turn an idea
into a successful product.

00:00:13.572 --> 00:00:17.356
Do something you're very passionate about.

00:00:17.356 --> 00:00:20.856
And don't try to chase what is kind of the

00:00:22.302 --> 00:00:24.651
hot passion of the day.

00:00:24.651 --> 00:00:25.484
(whoosh)

00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:27.777
- He's best known as the
founder, chairman, and CEO

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:29.373
of Amazon.com.

00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:31.353
He's also the founder of Blue Origin,

00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:33.263
a human space flight company.

00:00:33.263 --> 00:00:35.286
He's currently listed as
the third wealthiest person

00:00:35.286 --> 00:00:39.672
in the world, with a net
worth of $72.8 billion.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:40.591
He's Jeff Bezos,

00:00:40.591 --> 00:00:43.167
and here's my take on his
Top Ten Rules of Success,

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.153
Volume Two.

00:00:44.153 --> 00:00:45.035
Also guys, as you're watching,

00:00:45.035 --> 00:00:46.739
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:48.708
please leave it down
in the comments below,

00:00:48.708 --> 00:00:49.767
and put quotes around it,

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.084
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.647
Also when you write it down,

00:00:52.647 --> 00:00:56.264
it's much more likely to
lock into yourself as well.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:57.097
Enjoy.

00:00:57.097 --> 00:00:59.672
(whoosh)

00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:02.839
(inspirational music)

00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:09.528
(whoosh)

00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:13.488
- My job, one of my jobs
as the leader of Amazon,

00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:16.321
is to encourage people to be bold.

00:01:17.654 --> 00:01:21.524
And people love to focus on
things that aren't yet working.

00:01:21.524 --> 00:01:23.602
And that's good, that's human nature,

00:01:23.602 --> 00:01:26.522
that kind of divine discontent
can be very helpful.

00:01:26.522 --> 00:01:30.689
But it's incredibly hard to
get people to take bold bets.

00:01:32.393 --> 00:01:33.692
And you need to encourage that.

00:01:33.692 --> 00:01:35.799
And if you're going to take bold bets,

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:37.117
they're going to be experiments.

00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:38.475
And if they're experiments,

00:01:38.475 --> 00:01:41.106
you don't know ahead of time
that they're going to work.

00:01:41.106 --> 00:01:45.154
Experiments are, by their
very nature, prone to failure.

00:01:45.154 --> 00:01:48.628
But big successes, a few big successes,

00:01:48.628 --> 00:01:52.340
compensate for dozens and dozens
of things that didn't work.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.590
So bold bets AWS, Kindle, Amazon Prime,

00:01:57.121 --> 00:01:59.118
our third party seller business.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:03.285
All of those things are examples
of bold bets that did work,

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:06.445
and they pay for a lot of experiments.

00:02:06.445 --> 00:02:11.068
I've made billions of dollars
of failures at Amazon.com.

00:02:11.068 --> 00:02:14.651
Literally, billions of
dollars of failures.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.657
You might remember Pets.com, or Cozmo.

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:25.705
I'd give myself a root canal
with no anesthesia very easily.

00:02:25.705 --> 00:02:27.503
None of those things are fun.

00:02:27.503 --> 00:02:29.753
But they also don't matter.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.219
What really matters,

00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:35.248
is companies that don't
continue to experiment,

00:02:35.248 --> 00:02:38.295
companies that don't embrace failure,

00:02:38.295 --> 00:02:41.475
they eventually get in
the desperate position,

00:02:41.475 --> 00:02:43.958
where the only thing they can do

00:02:43.958 --> 00:02:45.334
is make a kind of Hail Mary bet

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:47.710
at the very end of their
corporate existence.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.559
Whereas companies that are, you know,

00:02:49.559 --> 00:02:53.411
making bets all along, even big bets,

00:02:53.411 --> 00:02:55.379
but not a bet the company bets.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:57.619
I don't believe in bet the company bets.

00:02:57.619 --> 00:02:58.805
That's when you're desperate.

00:02:58.805 --> 00:03:01.147
That's the last thing you can do.

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:01.980
(whoosh)

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.400
We know, from our past experiences,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.976
that big things start small.

00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:10.797
The biggest oak starts from an acorn.

00:03:10.797 --> 00:03:14.094
And if you want to do anything new,

00:03:14.094 --> 00:03:16.440
you've got to be willing
to let that acorn grow

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.964
into a little sapling, and
then finally into a small tree,

00:03:18.964 --> 00:03:23.556
and then maybe one day, it'll
be a big business on its own.

00:03:23.556 --> 00:03:25.428
- And in fact, that's one of your mottos

00:03:25.428 --> 00:03:26.527
for one of your initiatives.

00:03:26.527 --> 00:03:28.527
And forgive my pronunciation in Latin,

00:03:28.527 --> 00:03:30.476
but gradatim ferociter.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:31.869
What does that mean to you?

00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:36.097
- Well, it means step by step ferociously.

00:03:36.097 --> 00:03:39.014
And it's the motto for Blue Origin.

00:03:41.007 --> 00:03:44.046
Basically, you can't skip steps,

00:03:44.046 --> 00:03:46.027
you have to put one foot
in front of the other,

00:03:46.027 --> 00:03:50.164
things take time, there are no shortcuts.

00:03:50.164 --> 00:03:54.331
But you want to do those steps
with passion and ferocity.

00:03:57.149 --> 00:03:57.982
(whoosh)

00:03:57.982 --> 00:03:59.129
I think probably our most important piece

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:02.553
of intellectual property
is our brand name.

00:04:02.553 --> 00:04:05.204
And I think this is very important

00:04:05.204 --> 00:04:06.868
for anybody who's going
to start a company,

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:10.283
or marketing invention to understand,

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:12.925
is that brands for companies are like

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:14.862
reputations for people.

00:04:14.862 --> 00:04:18.596
And reputations are
hard-earned and easily lost

00:04:18.596 --> 00:04:21.043
So, the most important
intellectual property

00:04:21.043 --> 00:04:22.805
that a company can have,

00:04:22.805 --> 00:04:24.722
is for us, it's Amazon.

00:04:25.863 --> 00:04:28.366
It's that name, but what it stands for.

00:04:28.366 --> 00:04:30.989
We work very hard to earn trust.

00:04:30.989 --> 00:04:32.276
You can't ask for trust,

00:04:32.276 --> 00:04:34.860
you just have to do it the
hard way, one step at a time.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.839
You make a promise, and
then fulfill the promise.

00:04:37.839 --> 00:04:41.728
You say "we'll deliver
this to you tomorrow,"

00:04:41.728 --> 00:04:43.660
and we actually deliver it tomorrow.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.709
(laughing)

00:04:45.709 --> 00:04:49.105
And if you do that over and over again,

00:04:49.105 --> 00:04:52.458
then it ultimately, you can
instill your company's name

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.172
with the reputation.

00:04:54.172 --> 00:04:57.062
And that's, I think, sometimes
people talk about brands

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:58.516
in this very amorphous way.

00:04:58.516 --> 00:05:01.882
But for me, I like to
think of it as a person,

00:05:01.882 --> 00:05:03.872
and what is the reputation
that that person has,

00:05:03.872 --> 00:05:05.661
and how have they earned that reputation?

00:05:05.661 --> 00:05:06.494
(whoosh)

00:05:06.494 --> 00:05:09.213
I think stress, you can be,

00:05:09.213 --> 00:05:12.360
one of the things that's very
important to note about stress

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.242
is that stress primarily
comes from not taking action

00:05:17.242 --> 00:05:21.072
over something that you
can have some control over.

00:05:21.072 --> 00:05:24.673
So, if I find that some particular thing

00:05:24.673 --> 00:05:26.725
is causing me to have stress,

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:29.142
that's a warning flag for me.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.602
What it means is, there's something

00:05:32.602 --> 00:05:34.503
that I haven't completely identified,

00:05:34.503 --> 00:05:36.662
perhaps in my conscious mind,

00:05:36.662 --> 00:05:39.095
that is bothering me,

00:05:39.095 --> 00:05:40.985
and I haven't yet taken any action on it.

00:05:40.985 --> 00:05:43.443
I find, as soon as I identify it,

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.677
and make the first phone call,

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.489
or send off the first email message,

00:05:46.489 --> 00:05:48.606
or whatever it is that we're going to do

00:05:48.606 --> 00:05:50.596
to start to address that situation,

00:05:50.596 --> 00:05:52.496
even if it's not solved,

00:05:52.496 --> 00:05:54.092
the mere fact that we're addressing it,

00:05:54.092 --> 00:05:57.110
dramatically reduces any
stress that might come from it.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:00.417
So stress comes from ignoring things

00:06:00.417 --> 00:06:04.138
that you shouldn't be ignoring,
I think in large part.

00:06:04.138 --> 00:06:05.928
So stress doesn't come.

00:06:05.928 --> 00:06:10.327
People get stress wrong all
the time, in my opinion.

00:06:10.327 --> 00:06:13.789
Stress doesn't come from
hard work, for example.

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:16.856
You can be working incredibly
hard, and loving it.

00:06:16.856 --> 00:06:19.204
Likewise, you can be out of work,

00:06:19.204 --> 00:06:21.954
and incredibly stressed, over that.

00:06:21.954 --> 00:06:25.428
And likewise, if you kind
of use that as an analogy

00:06:25.428 --> 00:06:27.212
for what I was just talking about,

00:06:27.212 --> 00:06:28.209
if you're out of work,

00:06:28.209 --> 00:06:31.862
but you're going through
a disciplined approach

00:06:31.862 --> 00:06:34.774
of a series of job interviews and so on

00:06:34.774 --> 00:06:37.079
and are working to remedy that situation,

00:06:37.079 --> 00:06:38.774
you're going to be a lot less stressed

00:06:38.774 --> 00:06:41.612
than if you're just worrying
about it and doing nothing.

00:06:41.612 --> 00:06:42.445
(whoosh)

00:06:42.445 --> 00:06:44.364
If you're giving great
customer experience,

00:06:44.364 --> 00:06:48.447
and the only way to do
that is with happy people.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.617
You can't do it with a
set of miserable people,

00:06:55.883 --> 00:06:57.864
watching the clock all day.

00:06:57.864 --> 00:06:59.704
- So does that include work-life balance,

00:06:59.704 --> 00:07:00.537
and all those things?

00:07:00.537 --> 00:07:04.704
- Yes, but I teach three
leadership classes a year

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:07.961
at Amazon, well part of
it, they're bigger classes

00:07:07.961 --> 00:07:10.700
but I come in and teach a session.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.306
And I always talk about work-life balance,

00:07:13.306 --> 00:07:16.720
except I like to use the
phrase work-life harmony,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:17.610
rather than balance,

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:21.443
because to me, balance
implies a strict trade,

00:07:23.204 --> 00:07:27.037
whereas, I find that,
when I am happy at work,

00:07:28.608 --> 00:07:32.024
I come home more energized,
I'm a better husband,

00:07:32.024 --> 00:07:33.607
a better dad.

00:07:33.607 --> 00:07:38.509
And when I'm happy at home,
I come in a better boss,

00:07:38.509 --> 00:07:40.259
and better colleague.

00:07:44.515 --> 00:07:46.702
You can be out of work,

00:07:46.702 --> 00:07:49.702
and have terrible work-life balance.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:52.396
Even though you've got
all the time in the world,

00:07:52.396 --> 00:07:55.853
you can just feel like, oh my
god, you know, I'm miserable,

00:07:55.853 --> 00:07:57.832
and you would be draining energy.

00:07:57.832 --> 00:07:59.940
And so you have to find that harmony,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.415
it's a much better word.

00:08:01.415 --> 00:08:03.851
And I think for most people,

00:08:03.851 --> 00:08:05.523
it's about meaning.

00:08:05.523 --> 00:08:07.729
People want to know that
they're doing something

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:09.671
interesting and useful.

00:08:09.671 --> 00:08:10.504
And for us,

00:08:10.504 --> 00:08:14.935
because of the challenges that
we have chosen for ourselves,

00:08:14.935 --> 00:08:17.310
we get to work in the future.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.289
And it's super fun to work in the future,

00:08:19.289 --> 00:08:20.339
for the right kind of person.

00:08:20.339 --> 00:08:21.172
(whoosh)

00:08:21.172 --> 00:08:22.685
It's easy to have ideas.

00:08:22.685 --> 00:08:26.745
It's very hard to turn an idea
into a successful product.

00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:28.316
There are a lot of steps in between,

00:08:28.316 --> 00:08:31.782
it takes persistence, relentlessness.

00:08:31.782 --> 00:08:33.940
So, I always tell people who

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:36.394
think they want to be entrepreneurs,

00:08:36.394 --> 00:08:40.561
you need a combination of
stubborn relentlessness,

00:08:43.502 --> 00:08:44.835
and flexibility.

00:08:45.719 --> 00:08:47.728
And you have to know when to be which.

00:08:47.728 --> 00:08:50.967
And basically you need to
be stubborn on your vision,

00:08:50.967 --> 00:08:53.900
because otherwise it'll
be too easy to give up.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.059
But you need to be very
flexible on the details.

00:08:57.059 --> 00:08:59.631
Because as you go along
pursuing your vision,

00:08:59.631 --> 00:09:02.551
you'll find that some of your
pre-conceptions were wrong.

00:09:02.551 --> 00:09:04.918
And you're going to need to be
able to change those things.

00:09:04.918 --> 00:09:08.085
So I think taking an idea successfully

00:09:09.035 --> 00:09:10.678
all the way to the market

00:09:10.678 --> 00:09:13.004
and turning it into a real
product that people care about

00:09:13.004 --> 00:09:15.102
really improves people's lives.

00:09:15.102 --> 00:09:16.537
It's a lot of hard work.

00:09:16.537 --> 00:09:17.370
(whoosh)

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.865
I had some family role models,
and I had some other people,

00:09:19.865 --> 00:09:22.001
you know some sort of
historical role models

00:09:22.001 --> 00:09:23.406
that I looked at too.

00:09:23.406 --> 00:09:27.573
So certainly my grandfather was
a serious role model for me.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:29.753
I just had spent so much time,

00:09:29.753 --> 00:09:31.771
you learn different
things from grandparents

00:09:31.771 --> 00:09:33.196
than you learn from parents.

00:09:33.196 --> 00:09:36.641
I would encourage anybody
to try to spend time

00:09:36.641 --> 00:09:39.413
not only with their parents,
but with their grandparents.

00:09:39.413 --> 00:09:43.580
And I also had two people
I always would read about

00:09:46.271 --> 00:09:49.271
were Thomas Edison, and Walt Disney.

00:09:50.635 --> 00:09:54.015
Those were sort of my
two biographical heroes.

00:09:54.015 --> 00:09:55.611
(laughing)

00:09:55.611 --> 00:09:58.991
I've always been interested
in inventors and invention.

00:09:58.991 --> 00:10:02.741
And Edison, of course,
just for a little kid,

00:10:03.772 --> 00:10:07.028
and probably for adults too, I
still feel this way at least,

00:10:07.028 --> 00:10:11.135
is not only the symbol of that,
but the actual fact of that.

00:10:11.135 --> 00:10:13.552
The just incredible inventor.

00:10:15.905 --> 00:10:19.714
I've always felt that
there's a certain kind of

00:10:19.714 --> 00:10:21.841
important pioneering that goes on

00:10:21.841 --> 00:10:24.465
from an inventor like Thomas Edison.

00:10:24.465 --> 00:10:27.859
And then Disney was a
different sort of thing.

00:10:27.859 --> 00:10:32.292
He also, a real pioneer and
inventor, and doing new things.

00:10:32.292 --> 00:10:36.955
But it seemed to me that he
had this incredible capability

00:10:36.955 --> 00:10:39.252
to create a vision,

00:10:39.252 --> 00:10:41.717
that he could get a large
number of people to share.

00:10:41.717 --> 00:10:44.380
Because the things that Disney invented,

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.329
like Disneyland, the
theme parks, and so on,

00:10:47.329 --> 00:10:49.419
they were such big visions,

00:10:49.419 --> 00:10:51.232
that no single individual,

00:10:51.232 --> 00:10:54.039
unlike a lot of the things
that Edison worked on,

00:10:54.039 --> 00:10:57.374
no single individual
could ever pull them off.

00:10:57.374 --> 00:11:01.454
And Walt Disney really was able
to get a big team of people

00:11:01.454 --> 00:11:03.257
working in a concerted direction.

00:11:03.257 --> 00:11:04.090
(whoosh)

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:05.620
I don't really remember
the exact day or anything,

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:08.649
but when I was in college, is
when I started thinking about

00:11:08.649 --> 00:11:11.748
wanting to be an entrepreneur someday.

00:11:11.748 --> 00:11:15.330
So I was not the kid
with the lemonade stand.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.498
I wasn't one of these kids

00:11:17.498 --> 00:11:18.874
who was always trying to raise money.

00:11:18.874 --> 00:11:20.827
I always wanted to be a
scientist when I was little.

00:11:20.827 --> 00:11:23.148
But I'd also always loved computers.

00:11:23.148 --> 00:11:25.655
I was lucky, 'cause at
my age this was unusual

00:11:25.655 --> 00:11:27.950
to have access to a mainframe computer

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:30.903
from my elementary school
when I was in 4th grade,

00:11:30.903 --> 00:11:33.855
and quickly learned that
there was a pre-programmed

00:11:33.855 --> 00:11:35.618
Star Trek game on that computer,

00:11:35.618 --> 00:11:37.417
so I never did anything
except play Star Trek

00:11:37.417 --> 00:11:40.030
with that computer, so I don't
know how formative that was.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.446
It certainly helped my Star
Trek knowledge considerably.

00:11:43.446 --> 00:11:44.295
(audience laughing)

00:11:44.295 --> 00:11:46.998
But I've always loved computers.

00:11:46.998 --> 00:11:49.761
Somewhere in college, I started
watching some of the people

00:11:49.761 --> 00:11:54.680
who were setting up college
pizza delivery services,

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.055
and kind of the core entrepreneurs,

00:11:57.055 --> 00:11:58.234
and thinking, you know, this looks like

00:11:58.234 --> 00:11:59.549
a really fun thing to do.

00:11:59.549 --> 00:12:00.382
(whoosh)

00:12:00.382 --> 00:12:01.778
Science is a very rare idea,

00:12:01.778 --> 00:12:03.983
that can be done by a single individual.

00:12:03.983 --> 00:12:07.398
Almost everything that is
going to change the world,

00:12:07.398 --> 00:12:10.179
solve a problem, improve something,

00:12:10.179 --> 00:12:12.001
these are usually big efforts,

00:12:12.001 --> 00:12:15.247
and they require a team working together

00:12:15.247 --> 00:12:17.474
to really get something important done.

00:12:17.474 --> 00:12:20.494
And that has been the story of Amazon.com.

00:12:20.494 --> 00:12:23.433
Every step along the way,
we've had a team here

00:12:23.433 --> 00:12:25.516
that is making this work.

00:12:26.907 --> 00:12:29.157
Even at the smallest scale,

00:12:30.494 --> 00:12:32.512
you have to figure out how to
get help from your friends,

00:12:32.512 --> 00:12:36.400
from your family members,
from people that you can hire

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.816
in those early days.

00:12:37.816 --> 00:12:39.755
I think without that, it would never work.

00:12:39.755 --> 00:12:40.588
(whoosh)

00:12:40.588 --> 00:12:44.438
Do something you're very passionate about.

00:12:44.438 --> 00:12:48.605
And don't try to chase what
is the hot passion of the day.

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:52.752
I think we actually saw this,

00:12:52.752 --> 00:12:54.316
I think you see it all over the place

00:12:54.316 --> 00:12:55.613
in many different contexts.

00:12:55.613 --> 00:13:00.213
I think we saw it in the
Internet world quite a bit,

00:13:00.213 --> 00:13:04.296
where, at the sort of peak
of the Internet mania,

00:13:06.129 --> 00:13:07.931
in say 1999,

00:13:07.931 --> 00:13:12.037
we found people who were very passionate,

00:13:12.037 --> 00:13:14.456
somebody that kind of left that job,

00:13:14.456 --> 00:13:16.828
and decided I'm going to do
something in the Internet.

00:13:16.828 --> 00:13:20.995
Because it's almost like the
1849 gold rush, in a way.

00:13:22.331 --> 00:13:25.845
You find that people, you go
back and study the history

00:13:25.845 --> 00:13:27.013
of the 1849 gold rush.

00:13:27.013 --> 00:13:29.240
You find that at that time,

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.268
everybody who was within
shouting distance of California

00:13:33.268 --> 00:13:35.171
was, they might have been a doctor,

00:13:35.171 --> 00:13:36.269
but they quit being a doctor,

00:13:36.269 --> 00:13:37.693
and they started panning for gold.

00:13:37.693 --> 00:13:39.943
(laughing)

00:13:40.802 --> 00:13:43.135
And that almost never works.

00:13:45.682 --> 00:13:49.108
Even if it does work,
according to some metric,

00:13:49.108 --> 00:13:51.135
financial success, or
whatever it might be.

00:13:51.135 --> 00:13:54.511
I suspect it leaves you
ultimately unsatisfied.

00:13:54.511 --> 00:13:59.252
So you really need to be
very clear with yourself,

00:13:59.252 --> 00:14:01.717
and I think one of the
best ways to do that,

00:14:01.717 --> 00:14:05.873
is this notion of projecting
yourself forward to age 80,

00:14:05.873 --> 00:14:07.622
looking back on your life,

00:14:07.622 --> 00:14:09.111
and trying to make sure you've minimized

00:14:09.111 --> 00:14:10.380
the number of regrets you have.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.168
That works for career decisions,

00:14:13.168 --> 00:14:15.147
it works for family decisions.

00:14:15.147 --> 00:14:17.314
I have a 14-month-old son,

00:14:19.453 --> 00:14:21.395
and it's very easy for me to,

00:14:21.395 --> 00:14:23.433
if I think about myself when I'm 80,

00:14:23.433 --> 00:14:26.641
I know I want to watch
that little guy grow up.

00:14:26.641 --> 00:14:30.303
I don't want to be 80 and think shoot,

00:14:30.303 --> 00:14:32.040
I missed that whole thing,

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.231
and I don't have the kind
of relationship with my son

00:14:34.231 --> 00:14:36.775
that I wished I had, and so on and so on.

00:14:36.775 --> 00:14:38.190
So if you think about that,

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:41.407
so I guess another thing that
I would recommend to people,

00:14:41.407 --> 00:14:44.128
is that they always take
a long-term point of view.

00:14:44.128 --> 00:14:46.178
And I think this is something about which

00:14:46.178 --> 00:14:48.144
there's a lot of controversy.

00:14:48.144 --> 00:14:51.068
There's something a lot of people

00:14:51.068 --> 00:14:53.137
and I'm just not one of them,

00:14:53.137 --> 00:14:54.928
believe that you should live for the now.

00:14:54.928 --> 00:14:56.363
I think what you do,

00:14:56.363 --> 00:15:00.957
is you think about the great
expansive time ahead of you,

00:15:00.957 --> 00:15:03.115
and try to make sure that
you're planning for that

00:15:03.115 --> 00:15:06.777
in a way that is going to
leave you ultimately satisfied.

00:15:06.777 --> 00:15:09.477
This is just my, this is
the way it works for me.

00:15:09.477 --> 00:15:13.144
Everybody needs to find
that for themselves.

00:15:13.983 --> 00:15:18.339
So, I think there are a lot
of paths to satisfaction.

00:15:18.339 --> 00:15:21.763
And you need to find
one that works for you.

00:15:21.763 --> 00:15:23.846
(whoosh)

00:15:24.798 --> 00:15:25.631
- Thank you guys
(inspirational music)

00:15:25.631 --> 00:15:26.464
so much for watching,

00:15:26.464 --> 00:15:27.831
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video?

00:15:27.831 --> 00:15:28.999
And in general what do you think

00:15:28.999 --> 00:15:30.327
of our Volume Two series?

00:15:30.327 --> 00:15:31.693
Leave it down in comments below,

00:15:31.693 --> 00:15:33.573
I'm super curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:15:33.573 --> 00:15:36.137
Also I'd love to learn, what
did you take from this video?

00:15:36.137 --> 00:15:37.632
What did you learn from Jeff today?

00:15:37.632 --> 00:15:39.592
Which clip resonated the most with you,

00:15:39.592 --> 00:15:42.225
and what are you going to
immediately apply to your life

00:15:42.225 --> 00:15:43.434
or your business somehow?

00:15:43.434 --> 00:15:45.182
Please leave it down in the comments,

00:15:45.182 --> 00:15:46.766
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:15:46.766 --> 00:15:47.928
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:15:47.928 --> 00:15:48.886
to Carolyn Owens.

00:15:48.886 --> 00:15:51.591
Thank you so much, Carolyn, for
picking up a copy of my book

00:15:51.591 --> 00:15:55.005
"Your One Word," and posting
a picture of it on Twitter.

00:15:55.005 --> 00:15:56.604
I really really really
appreciate the support,

00:15:56.604 --> 00:15:58.112
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:15:58.112 --> 00:15:59.587
So thank you guys so much for watching,

00:15:59.587 --> 00:16:02.742
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:16:02.742 --> 00:16:04.847
and whatever your one word is.

00:16:04.847 --> 00:16:07.288
Much love, and I'll see you soon.

00:16:07.288 --> 00:16:09.371
(whoosh)

00:16:13.329 --> 00:16:17.612
- The best defense to
speech that you don't like

00:16:17.612 --> 00:16:19.759
about yourself, as a public figure,

00:16:19.759 --> 00:16:22.028
is to develop thick skin.

00:16:22.028 --> 00:16:25.263
It's really the only effective defense,

00:16:25.263 --> 00:16:27.075
because you can't stop it.

00:16:27.075 --> 00:16:29.025
You know you are going
to be misunderstood.

00:16:29.025 --> 00:16:31.837
If you're doing anything
interesting in the world,

00:16:31.837 --> 00:16:33.293
you're going to have critics.

00:16:33.293 --> 00:16:36.508
If you absolutely can't tolerate critics,

00:16:36.508 --> 00:16:39.399
then don't do anything
new, or interesting.

00:16:39.399 --> 00:16:40.367
(audience laughing)

00:16:40.367 --> 00:16:42.417
And then you can insulate yourself.

00:16:42.417 --> 00:16:44.004
Then think how wonderful your life--

00:16:44.004 --> 00:16:45.465
- Is that the Bezos principle?

00:16:45.465 --> 00:16:46.298
- Yeah.

00:16:46.298 --> 00:16:48.088
Usually people, if you see something,

00:16:48.088 --> 00:16:49.632
I don't know, you're
kind of a public figure,

00:16:49.632 --> 00:16:51.673
you've probably, things have
probably been written about you

00:16:51.673 --> 00:16:53.266
that you didn't think were nice.

00:16:53.266 --> 00:16:54.099
- That's true.

00:16:54.099 --> 00:16:57.687
- And, my advice if
you came to me and said

00:16:57.687 --> 00:17:00.141
"Jeff, somebody wrote this and
it really hurt my feelings.

00:17:00.141 --> 00:17:02.004
What should I do?"

00:17:02.004 --> 00:17:06.221
I would say, go stand on a street corner.

00:17:06.221 --> 00:17:08.248
In a crowded urban area.

00:17:08.248 --> 00:17:10.675
And watch all the people walk by.

00:17:10.675 --> 00:17:12.999
And think about what
they're thinking about.

00:17:12.999 --> 00:17:15.605
I bet you none of those
people are thinking about you.

00:17:15.605 --> 00:17:17.216
If you stay on that street corner,

00:17:17.216 --> 00:17:20.058
and really in your mind, you
can do this thought experiment.

00:17:20.058 --> 00:17:22.039
Like, okay, there's a
woman who just walked by.

00:17:22.039 --> 00:17:23.684
What is she actually thinking about?

00:17:23.684 --> 00:17:27.897
Maybe what she's going to
cook for dinner that night.

00:17:27.897 --> 00:17:31.897
Or the argument that she had
with one of her employees.

00:17:31.897 --> 00:17:33.973
Or, whatever it is.

00:17:33.973 --> 00:17:35.606
It's not about us.

00:17:35.606 --> 00:17:37.341
- A lot of small book publishers,

00:17:37.341 --> 00:17:39.201
and other smaller companies,

00:17:39.201 --> 00:17:43.576
worry that the power of
Amazon, give them no chance.

00:17:43.576 --> 00:17:45.200
- You've got to earn
your keep in this world.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.734
When you invent something new,

00:17:47.734 --> 00:17:50.119
if customers come to the party,

00:17:50.119 --> 00:17:51.702
it's disruptive to the old way.

00:17:51.702 --> 00:17:54.829
- But I mean, there are areas
where your power's so great,

00:17:54.829 --> 00:17:58.117
and your margin, you're
prepared to make it so thin.

00:17:58.117 --> 00:17:59.690
You can drive people out of business,

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:01.613
and you have that kind of strength,

00:18:01.613 --> 00:18:06.106
and people worry, is Amazon
ruthless in their pursuit

00:18:06.106 --> 00:18:07.500
of market share?

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:11.667
- The Internet is disrupting
every media industry, Charlie.

00:18:12.509 --> 00:18:15.396
People can complain about that,

00:18:15.396 --> 00:18:17.661
but complaining is not a strategy.

00:18:17.661 --> 00:18:20.362
Amazon is not happening to book selling,

00:18:20.362 --> 00:18:22.874
the future is happening to book selling.

